Elixir counting — status: DONE, verified live against real gameplay (2026-08-15)

Files: perception/elixir_counter.py (both signals + combining logic), perception/label_elixir.py (adds a new value to the template library from the current test_screenshot.png, same pattern as label_hand.py).

Design, refined from the original roadmap plan: NOT pure pixel counting alone (the original plan) — combines two signals. The NUMBER readout (white digit(s) over the droplet, one of 11 possible values 0-10) is template-matched the same way card_detector.py matches cards, and is the trusted signal for the integer part. The 10-segment BAR next to it is measured by pixel fill (algorithmic, no templates needed) for the FRACTIONAL progress toward the next point — confirmed genuinely continuous (not a discrete per-segment indicator) by comparing two live captures ~15s apart during calibration and seeing the partial-fill width shrink proportionally, consistent with real elixir regen rather than a fixed "next up" highlight.

Bar geometry (BAR_LEFT_X=244.5, BAR_RIGHT_X=864.0, 10 equal segments) calibrated against a real 900x1600 capture by locating the segment tick-divider lines pixel-by-pixel — positions were identical across independent captures, so treated as fixed. Re-calibrate (same as hand_region.py) if LDPlayer's resolution setting ever changes.

Real bug found and fixed during calibration: the elixir NUMBER glyph is drawn directly on top of the bar's own leftmost ~90px (overlapping segments 1-2), so a naive left-to-right pixel scan misread the digit's white/black pixels as bar fill — harmless when true fill already extended past that point, but badly wrong at low elixir (a true reading of 0 came back as 0.66). Two-part fix: (1) classify "filled" pixels using sum(R,G,B) > threshold AND green channel < 200, since both real fill colors (solid pink, and the lighter in-progress glow) have low green while the white digit text doesn't; (2) scan the bar right-to-left with per-row majority voting (not averaging raw pixel colors across sample rows first, which was blending digit black/white into false-positive intermediate colors) — scanning from the right confirms real fill from the unambiguous side of the bar, clear of the digit overlay entirely except at very low elixir. Verified against real captures after the fix: true 0 → 0.11, true 3 → 3.32, true 4 → 4.65-4.66, true 10 (2x-elixir mode) → 10.00.

Digit template coverage: full, all 11 values (0-10) now have at least one real example in assets/elixir_templates/ after a single match run with auto_collect.py's self-labeling (2026-08-15) — some values (1, 5, 7) picked up several examples in that one match. A few landed in the wrong value's folder initially (self-labeling is bar-estimate-based, and the bar can briefly misread during transition animations — see limitation below); Duncan spotted and manually moved these, and no fix was needed beyond that.

Verified live via debug_live.py (2026-08-15): reads correctly during normal play. The one known gap is transition states — while a card is actively being selected/placed, the bar/number readout is briefly unreliable (mid-animation). Deliberately not solving this at the perception layer: once cycle-tracking and known elixir costs exist (Phase 4), the system can cross-check/bridge these brief gaps from game logic (a played card's known cost + timing) rather than needing pixel-perfect reads through every animation frame. Revisit only if this actually causes a downstream problem.

Auto-collection added (2026-08-15): auto_collect.py's existing per-match capture loop (originally cards-only) now also self-labels elixir digit examples in the same loop, no extra ADB calls. Manually typing python -m perception.label_elixir <value> with the right timing turned out to be impractical during real play (by the time you've read the value and typed the command, the value's often already changed) — auto-collection removes the timing problem entirely. Unlike cards, elixir can't bootstrap off its own template-match confidence (plenty of values may have zero templates on day one), so it self-labels off elixir_counter.py's BAR reading instead, which needs no templates at all: a frame is only auto-filed when the bar's continuous estimate is within ELIXIR_STABLE_MARGIN=0.12 of a whole number (i.e. we just ticked over to that value, not mid-regen), making the inferred integer label trustworthy even before any digit template exists for it. Much shorter tail than cards (11 possible values, most seen within a game or two), so there's no separate review-queue step — anything ambiguous is just skipped, not queued. python -m perception.label_elixir <value> still exists as a manual fallback but shouldn't normally be needed now.

Known limitation, not yet handled: no "are we actually in a match" check — running the reader against a menu/transition screen (e.g. the post-match results screen) produces a plausible-looking but meaningless number instead of failing loudly, since the digit-match confidence is low but the bar-fill measurement doesn't know it's looking at nonsense either. Not a problem for the perception module itself (Phase 5's scripted bot will presumably already know match-state from elsewhere), but worth a guard if elixir_counter.py is ever called standalone.

