Capture speed upgrade (adbnativeblitz) — status: DONE (2026-08-15)

Originally recommended deferring this; overridden by Duncan (better to do it once now than revisit timing assumptions in code built on top of it later). perception/capture.py rewritten around adbnativeblitz's AdbFastScreenshots: a background thread runs `adb shell screenrecord` as one continuous H.264 stream (decoded via PyAV) instead of repeated PNG screencaps, and screenshot() just returns whatever the most recently decoded frame is. External API (screenshot(), save()) deliberately unchanged, so every existing caller (auto_collect.py, debug_live.py, collect_cards.py, etc.) needed zero changes. Verified for real against this project's LDPlayer setup, not trusted blindly: ~54 distinct frames/sec raw capture throughput (vs ~1.5-2fps before), and screenshot() call latency down to ~0.5ms (vs ~550ms) since it's now just reading an already-decoded frame instead of blocking on an ADB round-trip. Color channels confirmed correct without conversion (unlike the old PIL-based path, which needed one — see that bug's earlier history). scrcpy wasn't needed as a fallback.

Follow-on fix, same day: the capture speedup exposed that CARD TEMPLATE MATCHING was actually the next bottleneck (~145ms/frame against the now-210-example library) and that debug_live.py's --interval default (0.5s, tuned for the old slow-capture regime) was silently throttling everything to ~2fps regardless of how fast capture got. Fixed both: card_detector.py's MATCH_SIZE lowered 128x128 -> 64x64 (verified first against real stored examples — discriminative margin between correct/wrong cards holds essentially unchanged down to 32x32, so no accuracy tradeoff, ~4x less matching time), and debug_live.py's default --interval lowered to 0.03s. Real measured result: ~35-38ms/frame end-to-end, ~27fps, confirmed live by Duncan during an actual match ("much better now").

Live debug tool split into two windows same day, per Duncan's feedback that overlaid text was hard to read against moving gameplay: "Live hand detection" now shows only color-coded box outlines (no text) for spatial confirmation, and a new "Debug panel" window (plain dark background, positioned to the right by default) shows every actual value in detail — per-slot card name + confidence, elixir number/bar/fraction, timing. Verified visually by Duncan.

Unit detection wired into debug_live.py once unit_detector.py existed (2026-08-15): board units now get the same green/red (ally/enemy) box treatment on the gameplay window, and a "UNITS" section in the panel lists each detection's name/side/confidence. Real cost: adds ~45-50ms/frame on top of card matching, dropping the realistic ceiling from ~27fps (cards+elixir only) to ~12fps with units on — accepted as worth it, seeing units live matters more here than the extra fps. Panel's timing line now breaks out capture/cards/units separately.

Unit tracking added same day, per Duncan's live-testing feedback: unit_detector.py runs completely stateless (no memory between frames), so a real unit's detection confidence dipping for a frame or two (animation, rotation, brief occlusion) made it vanish and reappear — which without tracking looks exactly like units repeatedly being placed and dying, not a detection-accuracy problem. Fixed with a new perception/unit_tracker.py: assigns persistent track IDs across frames via greedy nearest-neighbor matching (same card name + same side + within MATCH_DISTANCE_PX=90px), with a MAX_MISSED_FRAMES=8 grace period before a track is actually dropped, so brief flicker no longer resets identity. Verified with a synthetic test (not just trusted): a unit flickering out for 3 frames keeps the same track ID on reappearing; a genuinely new unit gets a new ID even in the same frame as an existing one; a unit missing longer than the grace period (10 frames) correctly starts a new ID rather than wrongly linking two separate appearances. Known accepted limitation: several identical units of the same card standing close together (e.g. Skeleton Army) can have track IDs swapped between frames — doesn't matter yet since nothing downstream needs per-individual continuity, only counts and new-vs-same distinction, both unaffected by an ID swap between two interchangeable units.

debug_live.py's gameplay window got a detail toggle same day too, per Duncan's request for "both" simple and detailed rather than being locked into one: press 'd' live (or --detailed at startup) to switch between boxes-only and boxes-with-labels. In detailed mode, units show a compact color-coded tag (card initials + track ID, e.g. "K3" = Knight #3, "MP7" = Mini P.E.K.K.A. #7) instead of a full name+confidence label — enough to visually confirm a unit's ID stays stable across frames without cluttering the view. Full detail always available in the Debug panel regardless of the gameplay window's mode.

Duncan tested live over real matches (2026-08-15): tracking is working ("seems to be better"), but noted the panel's unit list was still the raw per-frame read, which necessarily still flickers when a unit is briefly occluded — asked for two separate sections (moment-by-moment vs. calculated/actual), and asked directly whether that's even possible given the system has no way to distinguish "died" from "briefly hidden." Answered honestly: telling those apart for real isn't solvable from vision alone yet (would need death-animation recognition or the not-yet-built HP reader — all three of died/occluded/model-missed-it produce an identical "no detection" signal), but the specific two-section ask didn't require solving that — unit_tracker.py already tracks through occlusion via its grace period, it just wasn't being exposed as a distinct view. Added UnitTracker.current_state() (returns every tracked unit including ones currently coasting on the grace period, each flagged visible=True/False) alongside the existing update() (unchanged, still the raw per-frame read).

Two more rounds of live-testing feedback, same day, both addressed: (1) Duncan found the panel itself hard to follow — text scrolling and reflowing every time a unit appeared/disappeared, since line count = current unit count. Fixed with his own suggested approach: a fixed-size grid (SlotGrid class, 50 slots, 10x5) where each track_id gets a STABLE cell position for as long as it exists, freed for reuse once gone — so units no longer cause the layout to jump, only individual cells' content changes. Both LIVE and TRACKED sections now render as grids (compact color-coded tags like "K3" per cell, dimmed for presumed/unconfirmed) sharing the SAME slot assignment, so a given unit sits in the identical cell in both grids — comparing them directly shows "tracker still believes this exists (dim, in TRACKED) but wasn't detected this exact frame (blank in LIVE)" at a glance instead of reading text. Verified visually with representative fake data before trusting it (build_panel rendered standalone, not just assumed correct). (2) Duncan asked for a data log to hand back for offline flicker analysis, also noting it'll be useful later for cross-checking against placement/action detection once that exists. Added JSONL logging (one line/frame: hand, elixir, live units, tracked units with visible/missed/age) to logs/debug_live_<timestamp>.jsonl by default, --log/--no-log to override. Bug caught and fixed before it could bite for real: default file buffering held everything in memory until close(), so a hard-killed/crashed session (exactly when the log matters most) lost all data — fixed with line-buffered writes (buffering=1), verified by killing a test session the same way and confirming the log survived (78 lines written despite an abrupt kill, vs. 0 before the fix). Panel height dropped from 1150 to 760 as a side effect of the grid layout being far more compact than the scrolling text list it replaced.

Supply-chain due diligence on adbnativeblitz, done properly (2026-08-15) after Duncan asked: real, identifiable author (Johannes Fischer / "hansalemaos"), 868 public GitHub repos, 500 followers, active since 2021 — and specifically a genuine specialist in Android automation/emulation tooling (cyandroemu 165 stars, cyandrocel 70 stars, several other ADB/emulator repos, some with 2025 commits). adbnativeblitz itself: MIT licensed, 23 stars, 0 open issues, no security advisories, no CVEs/malware reports found. One real caveat: only 3 commits total, all within its 3-day creation window in Oct 2023 — unmaintained since, though with no sign anything's actually wrong, and we're already on its latest release (0.11). Verdict: checks out, proceeding as-is.

Unit-creation/spawn attribution — status: known issue, deliberately not being solved yet (2026-08-15)

Raised by Duncan: many cards spawn additional units as a side effect, not from a fresh hand-play — Lumberjack drops a Rage effect on death, Witch and Night Witch periodically spawn Skeletons/Bats while alive, Golem and Elixir Golem split into smaller copies of themselves on death, Goblin Giant spawns Spear Goblins on death, buildings like Barbarian Hut/Goblin Hut/Furnace/Tombstone periodically produce units for free over their lifetime, Skeleton King can summon skeletons via an active ability. Important distinction worked out during planning: the VISUAL recognition of these spawned sub-units is likely already fine — they mostly have their own classes in the 97-class model (e.g. "golemite" is already a separate class) — so this is not expected to complicate unit_detector.py itself. The real problem is one layer up: correctly attributing a newly-appeared unit to "a byproduct of an existing card's ability" instead of miscounting it as a fresh card played from the opponent's hand, which would corrupt cycle-tracking/elixir-inference (Phase 4). This is a game-knowledge problem, not a vision problem. Planned eventual fix: a static reference table (card name → spawn behavior: on-death splits/spawns, periodic while-alive production, spawn unit type + count), consulted by Phase 4's cycle-tracking logic. Not building this table or the attribution logic now — tackling it "down the road" per Duncan, once Phase 4 (cycle tracking) is actually being built.

Tower false-positive filtering — status: DONE, verified (2026-08-15)

Duncan ran two full matches with debug_live's logging on and noticed towers themselves (specifically the defender figures on top, visible while attacking) were being flagged as troops. Investigated the first log quantitatively before touching anything: 63.5% of all 381 track IDs created over a 101s match were seen only 1-2 times total (heavy noise), and cross-referencing bbox movement in the second log (after adding bbox to the log format) found two tracks — an "archer" and a "musketeer", both ally-side — sitting near-motionless (~4-5px avg movement) for 137-147 of 586 frames (23-25% of the entire match). Considered and ruled out the alternative explanation (a real, legitimately stationary defending troop) before concluding these were false positives, based on their combination of extreme persistence + near-zero movement being a much stronger signature than normal defensive troop behavior.

Fix: ground-truth calibration rather than guessing coordinates — Duncan clicked top-left/bottom-right corners of all 6 towers plus the river and both bridges via find_coords.py against a real live-match capture. Important correction made during this process: the river must NOT be a single blanket exclusion zone, since flying units legitimately cross it — only the two bridges are safe for GROUND troops. New config/red_zones.py holds TOWER_ZONES (always excluded, any unit) and WATER_ZONES (3 river segments with gaps at both bridges, GROUND-only exclusion). Verified visually before trusting it: drew all zones over the real capture and confirmed each tightly wraps its actual tower structure (including where defender figures stand) and the bridge gaps land exactly on the real wooden bridges.

Needed per-unit air/ground metadata to make the water-zone exclusion transport-aware, which unit_detector.py didn't have (only names were kept when it was built) — added new perception/unit_metadata.py, sourced from build-a-bot's namespaces/units.py (same MIT-licensed provenance as the detection model). Cross-checked against known game facts rather than copied blind: found and corrected one real error in the upstream data — Ice Wizard was listed as Transport.AIR, but it's a ground unit in the actual game. unit_detector.py's detect() now filters out any detection whose bbox CENTER falls in a tower zone (always) or a water zone (only if the unit's transport is ground, via unit_metadata.is_air()).

Verified against a live capture: the single-frame false positive that had been recurring near a tower was gone after the fix, with no other detections in that frame incorrectly suppressed. Noted a separate, different issue while checking this — a spell/barrel explosion effect near a bridge got misclassified as "goblin_hut" (a building) at 0.75 confidence; NOT related to the tower/water fix (correctly passed through, since it's not physically in a red zone) — this is the spell-vs-troop misclassification problem already documented under "Spell detection" above, not a new bug.

Second calibration pass, same day: Duncan ran another full match and reported towers were STILL getting flagged, plus a new observation — Arrows got briefly flagged as a troop when it landed. Investigated the log properly this time rather than re-guessing: checked whether any surviving detections fell inside the (already-filtering) zones — trivially zero, since the filter runs before logging, so that check alone proves nothing. Redid the static/persistent-track analysis from the first pass on the new log and cross-referenced each suspicious track's distance to the nearest tower zone: several sat just 2.5-16.5px outside a zone boundary (not inside it) — meaning the zones weren't broken, they were calibrated too small. Confirmed directly: captured a fresh live frame, cropped each tower with generous padding and pixel gridlines, and found the actual defending figure/apparatus standing on every tower type extends well above the tower's base structure — the original click-derived boxes only covered the base. Fixed by extending each zone's top edge (~75px for king towers, ~55px for princess towers) and re-verifying visually that the expanded boxes fully wrap each figure without growing so large they'd swallow a real nearby defending troop. Not yet re-confirmed with a full match log after this second fix.

Architecture correction, same day, per Duncan's own catch: the expanded (base + figure) boxes and the original click-derived (base-only) boxes answer two DIFFERENT questions that had been collapsed into one TOWER_ZONES list — "where can a troop physically stand" (a game fact, should eventually back placement validation and tile-grid reasoning) vs. "where should the detector suppress output" (a vision-pipeline patch specific to this model's failure mode). Using the larger detection-exclusion boxes for a future placement check would have wrongly rejected legal troop placements directly behind a tower, which is real walkable board area. Split config/red_zones.py into TOWER_FOOTPRINT_ZONES (the original tight clicks, for future game-logic use) and TOWER_DETECTION_EXCLUSION_ZONES (the expanded ones, for unit_detector.py's filtering only) with explicit docstrings on which is which and why neither should be substituted for the other. unit_detector.py's filter function renamed _is_impossible_position -> _should_suppress to stop implying the tower case is a true impossible-position fact the way the water case genuinely is.

Confirmed working (2026-08-15): Duncan ran a full match, saw zero princess-tower false positives. General flicker remained — investigated with the log rather than guessing further. Two findings: (1) confidence gap between noise and real tracks was stark (noise avg 0.502, real avg 0.744 conf); at MIN_CONF=0.4 only 6.7% of real tracks' own frames would dip below it (vs 13.3% at 0.5), a cost the tracker's existing grace period absorbs without losing identity. (2) 158 of 356 total track IDs in that match (44%) were matched exactly ONCE, ever — pure single-frame noise blips.

Fixed both: raised unit_detector.py's MIN_CONF 0.3 -> 0.4 (build-a-bot's original default was 0.3). Added a confirmation-delay to unit_tracker.py (MIN_HITS_TO_CONFIRM=2, new): a track isn't reported anywhere (neither update()'s return nor current_state()) until matched twice — kills single-frame blips outright without touching how an already-established track behaves (missed_frames/grace-period logic untouched, only affects a track's first appearance). Verified with a synthetic test before trusting it: a one-off blip never appears at all; a real 2-hit track appears starting from its 2nd hit; current_state() correctly shows confirmed tracks only.

Re-tested live over a fresh ~100s match (comparable length to the match that produced the original 356-track baseline): total unique track IDs dropped 356 -> 110 (-69%), the proportion of long-lived/"real" tracks (>8 appearances) more than doubled (19.9% -> 43.6%), and single-appearance noise dropped from 44% to 20% of all tracks. Real, measured improvement — not a complete fix. Some noise remains (confirmed detections that still only last 2-3 frames before vanishing for good exist), consistent with what Duncan's own live impression suggested ("still a lot of flickering... not sure how much we can do about that now") — likely at or near the practical ceiling for a single-frame detector without a fundamentally different signal (e.g. HP tracking, per Project_Status's HP-reading plan above) to cross-check against.

The Arrows observation is a SEPARATE, different bug, not something red-zones can fix — a spell landing can happen anywhere on the board, not at a fixed position, so there's no static zone to exclude it with. This is the same spell-vs-troop misclassification category as the goblin_hut/barrel finding above (see "Spell detection" status) — the real fix is the planned dedicated spell-detection module, not more red zones.

Phase 4 — cycle tracking — status: own-side DONE and verified live (2026-08-16); opponent card-play detection (troops/buildings) built and first-live-tested same day, two real bugs found and fixed (see below), fixes validated against the problem log but not yet re-verified with a fresh live run; opponent deck/hand/elixir modeling not started

Sequencing decided with Duncan: own-side cycle tracking first, opponent-side modeling second — deliberately in that order, not in parallel. Own-side is fully tractable right now: the 8-card deck list, live hand detection, live elixir count, and the game's own on-screen "Next:" card preview already give everything needed, no new perception work required. Opponent-side is inherently harder (we never see their hand, so it's probabilistic inference, not direct observation) — building it second means it gets a proven pattern and a ground-truth-verified foundation (the own-side tracker) to build on, rather than tackling the hardest version of the problem first.

Own cycle tracker — status: built, not yet verified live (2026-08-15): tracks which of the 8 deck cards are currently in hand (already known live) and, by elimination, the order of the other 4. Cross-references card plays against known elixir costs (config/cards.json) to get near-certain confidence about when and what we played. This is the concrete mechanism Duncan identified for using logic to route around the vision-level flicker documented above — a hand-slot change plus a matching elixir drop is a far stronger signal than anything a single detection frame offers. It's also the direct prerequisite for finally tackling the deferred unit-spawn-attribution problem (Witch/Golem/Goblin Giant etc. — see "Unit-creation/spawn attribution" above), which has been waiting specifically on this.

Correction to this doc's earlier assumption: the plan above originally said tracking would use "the visible 'next' queue slot" the game shows on-screen. Checked before building, and that slot isn't actually perception-supported — config/hand_region.py only defines the 4 hand slots, nothing for a 5th "next" region. Turned out not to matter: cycle order is fully derivable without ever reading that UI element, by elimination alone — every time a hand slot's confirmed card changes, whichever new card appears there IS the queue's front card, revealed after the fact instead of ahead of time. So the "no new perception work needed" conclusion was still right, just for a different reason than originally assumed.

New module: game_knowledge/cycle_tracker.py (new game_knowledge/ package, per the roadmap's suggested layout). Design: back_pool (the set of original deck cards not yet placed in hand or revealed via a play — order genuinely unknown until each surfaces) + tail_queue (a FIFO of cards already played once this match, in the exact order played — fully known in real time). This split matches the actual game rule that unresolved original cards must all surface once before any newly-requeued card can resurface. back_pool always empties after the first four real plays (one "lap"), after which tail_queue alone is the queue and the rest of the match is fully deterministic — verified with a manual step-by-step simulation (not just assumed correct): initial hand fill, four plays cycling through all of back_pool, confirming queue_fully_resolved flips true at the right moment, then a fifth play correctly popping/rotating tail_queue on the second lap.

Debounce: a hand slot's card is only "confirmed" after HAND_STABLE_FRAMES=3 consecutive identical reads (higher than unit_tracker.py's MIN_HITS_TO_CONFIRM=2, since a wrongly-confirmed play corrupts cycle state, not just one frame's box) — avoids a template-match flicker or mid-animation blip firing a false play event. Elixir cross-check: on a candidate play, compares elixir from the start vs. end of a rolling ELIXIR_HISTORY_SECONDS=3.0s buffer against the played card's known cost, net of regen over the elapsed interval (config.game_constants.ELIXIR_REGEN_SECONDS["normal"] only — double/triple-elixir phases aren't compensated yet, a documented known limitation, not blocking). Within ELIXIR_MATCH_TOLERANCE=1.0 -> confirmed=True; outside it, the hand-state change is still recorded (it visibly happened) but flagged confirmed=False rather than silently trusted — verified both paths with a synthetic test (a real-looking elixir drop confirms, a deliberately-wrong one doesn't).

Real bug caught during testing, not just trusted: card name normalization. Built a _slug() function to map config/cards.json's display names (e.g. "Mini P.E.K.K.A") to elixir costs keyed the same way deck.txt entries are. First attempt reused perception/download_card_icons.py's safe_filename() on the assumption it was already the established convention — it silently produced the wrong slug for punctuation-heavy names ("mini_p_e_k_k_a" instead of "mini_pekka"), which would have made mini_pekka's cost lookup fail for real decks containing it. Caught by an actual test run (not by inspection) that hit a hand-crafted deck including mini_pekka; confirmed by checking both assets/card_icons/ (which genuinely does use safe_filename()'s literal output, "mini_p_e_k_k_a.png") and assets/hand_templates/ (which uses the hand-typed "mini_pekka" convention instead) directly rather than assuming either was right — these two parts of the codebase turn out to already disagree with each other on this, a pre-existing inconsistency, not something newly introduced. cycle_tracker.py's _slug() now strips periods/apostrophes before collapsing whitespace, matching the hand_templates/deck.txt convention specifically, and is documented as deliberately different from safe_filename() rather than presented as a shared helper.

Wired into perception/debug_live.py: a CycleTracker(deck) instance is created at startup, updated once per frame alongside cards/elixir, and reset manually via a new 'r' keypress (no automatic match-boundary detection exists anywhere in this project yet — a known, deliberately deferred gap, same one already noted under elixir counting above). Debug panel gets a new CYCLE section (queue order with "?" placeholders, last play + confirmed/unconfirmed flag); panel canvas height bumped 760->860px to fit it without crowding the existing unit grids. JSONL log schema extended with a "cycle" key per frame, for the same offline-analysis reason the log already exists.

First live test run (2026-08-15, match didn't finish — session ended early, not a tracker issue) surfaced two real bugs the hand-written simulation hadn't caught, found by replaying the actual JSONL log rather than trusting the design on paper:

Bug 1 — below-threshold guesses treated as real reads: debug_live.py's per-frame slot_results carries the card detector's best GUESS even when its score is below card_detector.CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD (debug_live.py only uses the score to pick a box color for a human, it never downgrades the name to "UNKNOWN" itself). cycle_tracker.update() was blindly trusting that name, so a low-confidence wrong guess during a real card's play-animation transition (observed for real: "giant" at ~0.3 confidence flickering for several frames while a genuine "knight" was mid-play) could pass the 3-frame debounce and get treated as a real card in hand. This was the direct cause of the nonsensical-looking panel Duncan flagged after the test — dozens of spurious "plays" registering within a couple of real seconds, nowhere near fast enough to be real. Fixed: update() now ignores any slot_results entry below CARD_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD itself, rather than assuming the caller pre-filtered.

Bug 2 — silent desync from trusting internal queue order over observation: once back_pool empties (first lap complete), _register_play used to always popleft() tail_queue's believed front and assume it equalled the observed revealed card, without checking. A single high-confidence WRONG read anywhere breaks that assumption — and did, for real, once in this same test log (a "knight" play revealed "archers", but the model believed "mini_pekka" was next) — and the old code had no way to notice: it silently popped the wrong card (mini_pekka, which was still legitimately sitting in hand), leaving archers duplicated into two queue positions at once and mini_pekka missing from the whole 8-card model for the rest of the match. Fixed: _register_play now always locates the observed revealed card within tail_queue directly and removes THAT (self-correcting to reality every play), setting a new desync=True flag on the play event whenever the model's belief didn't match — surfaced in the panel (magenta) and log rather than hidden, same "flag it, don't hide it" pattern as the elixir confirmed flag.

Both fixes verified by replaying the actual problem log through the corrected code with per-frame invariant assertions (no duplicate card across hand+queue, no missing card, hand∩queue always empty) — held for all 951 frames. Real outcome on that log: 12 real plays detected (down from dozens of noise-driven ones pre-fix), all elixir-confirmed, with exactly 1 desync flagged and correctly self-corrected — consistent with roughly one genuine high-confidence misdetection across a ~90s match, not a systemic problem.

Panel redesigned same session, per Duncan's feedback that the original single "queue" line (4 slots with "?" placeholders, no clear hand/queue separation) "made no sense" to read at a glance. Now shows the full 8-card picture in three explicit groups: HAND (4, numbered to match the visible UI slots), ON DECK (the single card that will be drawn next), and IN QUEUE (the remaining 3, in order) — directly mirroring the real game structure instead of one ambiguous list. JSONL log's "cycle" key updated to match (on_deck/rest_of_queue instead of a flat queue list).

Verified clean on a full, uninterrupted match (2026-08-16): 728 frames, 7 real plays detected, all elixir-confirmed, 0 desyncs, every per-frame invariant held (no duplicate/missing card across hand+queue, hand/queue never overlap) — checked by replaying the JSONL log with those invariants asserted every frame, not just eyeballing the panel. Queue reached full resolution by match end. The last ~1.7s of that log (after Duncan intentionally kept debug_live.py running a couple seconds past the match ending) showed the expected post-match-screen garbage — all four hand scores collapsed to 0.04-0.38, well under threshold, names bouncing randomly frame to frame — and correctly produced zero spurious plays, confirming Bug 1's fix (ignore sub-threshold reads) also covers this same "not actually in a match" case documented elsewhere in this file, without needing a dedicated match-state check yet.

Own-side cycle tracking status: DONE, verified live.

Opponent card-play detection (troops/buildings) — status: built (2026-08-16), not yet verified live

Per Duncan's explicit direction after own-side was solid: solidify accurate detection of WHEN the opponent plays a card and WHICH card, before attempting opponent deck/elixir inference on top of it (matches the philosophy doc §2's own stated priority — everything else about opponent modeling depends on getting this right first). Troops/buildings only; spells remain the separate, not-yet-built Tier 1/2/3 module described above.

Two new files: game_knowledge/spawn_data.py (static reference data) and game_knowledge/opponent_play_tracker.py (OpponentPlayTracker class). Builds directly on the existing, already-working perception/unit_detector.py + perception/unit_tracker.py — the only missing piece was correctly explaining what a NEW enemy track means, since it doesn't always mean a fresh hand play.

Finally built the spawn-attribution table Project_Status has been calling "deliberately deferred" since the "Unit-creation/spawn attribution" section above (Golem splitting on death, Witch/Tombstone/Goblin Hut/Barbarian Hut/Furnace producing units for free, etc.) — this was explicitly "waiting on" cycle tracking existing first, which it now does. Two real corrections came directly from Duncan before any code was written (more reliable than general CR knowledge, per the philosophy doc's own byline): Tombstone spawns skeletons, Goblin Hut spawns goblins — a first draft had wrongly guessed Goblin Hut produced Spear Goblins.

A structural gap Duncan caught in the first draft of the design (before implementation): several cards spawn MULTIPLE units in a single hand-play (Minions=3, Goblins=4, Skeleton Army=~15, Barbarians=5, Royal Recruits=6, Rascals=1+2, Bats=5, Fire Spirits=2, Guards=3) — the original one-track-at-a-time design would have miscounted one real play as several separate events. Fixed by grouping new same-side-same-class tracks that appear within GROUPING_WINDOW_SECONDS=0.5s into one candidate group before explaining it, rather than reacting to each track individually.

Real modeling bug caught and fixed before shipping (not just during later testing — caught while writing the code, by tracing through the musketeer case by hand): "musketeer" is the one class valid at TWO different fixed counts as two different real cards (1 = Musketeer, 3 = Three Musketeers). An early version of the logic treated it like every other multi-unit card (one fixed "expected" burst size), which would have flagged every ordinary single Musketeer play as "uncertain" — a real card being constantly misclassified, not a rare edge case. Fixed with a dedicated special case, same treatment "skeleton" already needed (3 = Skeletons, ~15 = Skeleton Army, or a 1-2 trickle from Tombstone/Witch/Skeleton King — three-way ambiguous by count alone, handled before the general multi-unit path).

Per Duncan's explicit ask that the system be able to flag when it's clearly reading something wrong rather than silently forcing a bad guess: when a new group's size matches neither a known card's burst count nor any known spawner, it's reported attribution="uncertain" with a flag_reason string explaining which rule it violated (e.g. "2 minion appeared together; minion cards always come in groups of 3"), shown in magenta in debug_live.py's panel rather than folded into "played" or "spawned". This is the vision-level piece of that ask — the deeper elixir-based version (a play that would require more elixir than the opponent could plausibly have) isn't built yet, since opponent elixir itself hasn't been built (it depends on this module's output, not the other way around) — but every event already carries elixir_cost when cheaply determinable, specifically so that future module can consume this one's output directly.

Verified so far with a synthetic simulation only (no live match run yet this session) covering all the cases above: common single-unit play, a 3-unit Minions burst grouping correctly into one event, Golem-death → golemite attribution, Tombstone → skeleton trickle attribution, Skeletons(3) vs Skeleton Army(~15) told apart by count, a malformed 2-minion group correctly flagged uncertain instead of guessed, and both Musketeer counts resolving to the right card + cost. All passed. Not yet run against a real match.

Known limitations, flagged rather than hidden (same pattern used throughout this file): can't distinguish a real on-death spawn from a track that merely stopped being detected (the same died-vs-occluded gap already documented under unit tracking); SPAWN_PROXIMITY_PX=150 is an unverified first-pass guess, not yet tuned against real footage; a single real burst can get split into two groups if its detections straggle past the 0.5s grouping window; goblin_giant isn't one of the 97 detected classes at all (confirmed by reading unit_detector.py's CLASS_NAMES directly), so its spear_goblin death-spawns can never be attributed to a source — not fixable without a different/retrained model.

First live-match test (2026-08-16, full match, log logs/debug_live_20260816_012809.jsonl, 1254 frames, 63 events) surfaced two real bugs and one UI request, all addressed same day:

Bug 1 (dominant): ally troops misattributed as enemy plays. Duncan's own read of the panel flagged this before the log was even opened — every one of the most-repeated "opponent" cards (giant 12x, musketeer 6x, archer ~14x, plus a few knight/minipekka) is exactly the PLAYER's own deck (config/deck.txt). Traced two concrete cases in the log: an ally Giant misclassified as enemy for its first ~2s on the board (an "enemy" giant track died and an "ally" giant track was born one frame later at a near-identical position), and a well-established ally Musketeer (alive 296 frames) that flipped to "enemy" for a few frames after an occlusion. Root cause: perception/unit_detector.py's SideDetector (side.onnx) returns a bare argmax label with no confidence exposed anywhere — the same class of bug already fixed once in cycle_tracker.py (trusting a per-frame classifier with no plausibility gate), showing up here in a new module.

Fixed with two independent cross-checks in game_knowledge/opponent_play_tracker.py, both producing a new 4th attribution value, "misclassified" (distinct from "uncertain" — this one most likely represents ZERO real enemy plays, not just an ambiguous one, which matters for a future opponent-elixir consumer that should exclude it entirely):
1. Ally-vanish proximity check: if a same-class ALLY track vanished very recently (SIDE_FLIP_WINDOW_SECONDS=1.0s) near a new enemy group's position, it's probably the same physical unit.
2. Own-play collision check, added after Duncan caught a real gap in the first version of fix 1 (only ~3 of the ~15+ suspected false positives were being caught, since many misclassifications happen at a track's BIRTH — no prior ally track to compare against at all): cross-references game_knowledge.cycle_tracker.py's own confirmed plays (fed in live from debug_live.py's cycle_state["last_play"] each frame). Duncan's own catch on this one: timing alone isn't safe, since a mirror/similar opponent deck could genuinely play the same card around the same time — a false suppression, not a fix. Made safe with a POSITION requirement using config/red_zones.py's already-calibrated river coordinates (reused, not reduplicated): only suppresses when the new group's position is NOT clearly on the opponent's own side of the board (i.e. at/past the river) — a genuine simultaneous opponent play can only ever first appear on their own side, so this can't false-positive on the mirror-deck case. Verified with a dedicated test (a same-card/same-timing collision positioned near the opponent's own tower correctly stays "played", not suppressed).

Re-running both fixes against the actual problem log (not just synthetic tests): misclassified count went 0 -> 3 (ally-vanish check alone) -> 5 (both checks together) of 63 total events, including the original Giant case that started the investigation. Real remaining gap, found and explicitly NOT chased further (a conscious stopping point, not an oversight): several musketeer/archer events still read as "played" even though Duncan doesn't believe the opponent had those cards — traced one (t=95.39) to the same birth-time misclassification pattern, but with NO nearby ally vanish AND no recent own-play of that exact card to correlate against (nearest own play was a different card), so neither check can catch it without loosening the windows enough to reintroduce real mirror-deck false-negative risk. Duncan's own proposed real fix, noted for later: improve the vision system's object permanence (planned unit HP reading, see "Unit HP reading" section above) specifically so death-vs-occlusion can be told apart — which is exactly the ambiguity underlying why an established unit's track identity gets lost and reborn (possibly misclassified) in the first place, not just a coincidence of this specific bug.

Bug 2 (real data gap): "Archers" was missing from CARD_BURST_COUNT — Archers is always exactly 2 units, no standalone single "Archer" card exists, but game_knowledge/spawn_data.py never had an entry for it, so a genuine 2-Archers play mis-flagged as "uncertain" instead of "played". Fixed (added "archer":2 and the "archer"->"archers" slug mapping). Also fixed while wiring the own-play collision check (needed a real slug match against cycle_tracker's own records, exposing this): "minipekka" (unit class) vs "mini_pekka" (real card slug) was never mapped, so a genuine solo Mini P.E.K.K.A. play would have gotten elixir_cost=None and failed to cross-reference against our own plays — fixed the same way (CARD_SLUG_FOR_BURST, despite the name now also covering non-burst slug mismatches found along the way).

heal_spirit / inferno_tower — no code fix, per Duncan's own read of the timing. Both appeared late (t=84-113s in a match that ran to t=121s) at implausible positions for real gameplay (heal_spirit's bbox is nearly frozen at [434-437, 80-121] across 29 seconds — a static screen location, not a moving unit; neither card is even in the player's deck so the ally-flip theory can't explain them either). Duncan's own hypothesis: likely low-consequence readings from right around when the match actually ended (post-game/victory-screen noise) — consistent with the "no match-state check" gap already documented elsewhere in this file. No new code needed.

UI: OPPONENT PLAYS changed from a rolling last-5 (replaced as new events arrived — Duncan: "adding and removing cards") to a full ordered, mouse-wheel-scrollable history, per Duncan's explicit request. perception/debug_live.py now keeps opponent_events_all for the whole match and a small scroll_state = {"offset": 0} updated by a cv2.setMouseCallback handler on the Debug panel window (offset=0 always shows the newest events; scrolling up pages into older history and stays there rather than snapping back down when new events arrive). Verified cv2's actual mouse-wheel API against this project's real cv2 build (5.0.0) rather than assumed: cv2.EVENT_MOUSEWHEEL exists, but the commonly-referenced cv2.getMouseWheelDelta() helper does not exist in this version — decoded the signed delta directly from the callback's flags argument instead (flags >> 16). "misclassified" events render in dark gray, distinct from played (green) / spawned (gray) / uncertain (magenta), so the mitigation's firing rate is visible at a glance.

Second live-match test (2026-08-16, partial match stopped early, log logs/debug_live_20260816_015913.jsonl) — Duncan's own read: "almost working worse than before." Musketeer and archers still flagged constantly despite (per Duncan) not being in the opponent's deck; the giant issue was still happening. Spear Goblins correctly stayed "uncertain" rather than being wrongly claimed (Goblin Giant's on-death spawn — matches the already-documented goblin_giant detection gap, working as intended).

Root cause of why the previous fix barely helped, found by tracing the SAME giant/musketeer pattern again in the new log: the ally-vanish check was diffing track_id SET MEMBERSHIP, but unit_tracker.py's own grace period keeps a track sitting in tracked_units (visible=False, "presumed") for up to MAX_MISSED_FRAMES frames after real detection stops — the track_id never actually leaves the dict during that window, so the set-membership diff never saw a "vanish" at all. Confirmed for real, twice: a giant (track id=1) and a musketeer (track id=17, alive 90+ frames) both flickered visible=True->False->True while KEEPING THE SAME track_id throughout, while a completely separate new "enemy" track of the same class was born and confirmed during the invisible window. The previous fix's checks never fired because they were watching the wrong signal (set membership) for the dominant real-world case.

Fixed: both the enemy-side (_recently_vanished, on-death spawn source) and ally-side (_recently_vanished_ally, misclassification source) tracking in game_knowledge/opponent_play_tracker.py's update() now ALSO catch a track's visible:True->False transition while it's still present in tracked_units, not just full removal from the dict — the dominant real case, not the edge case. Verified with a dedicated synthetic test reproducing the exact pattern (same track_id throughout, visible flickers False then True while a new same-class enemy track is born during the flicker) before trusting it, same rigor as every other fix this session.

Second bug found in the same investigation: SIDE_FLIP_WINDOW_SECONDS=1.0s was too tight relative to GROUPING_WINDOW_SECONDS's own 0.5s delay — the ally-vanish check only runs when a candidate group CLOSES, already 0.5s after the enemy track first appeared, on top of the real gap between the ally vanishing and the new enemy being confirmed. Traced a real case where the combined delay was ~1.06s — juuust outside the 1.0s window, so the ally-vanish record got pruned one frame before the check ran. Widened to 2.0s (matching RECENT_DEATH_WINDOW_SECONDS) — safe to widen freely here, unlike OWN_PLAY_WINDOW_SECONDS, since an ally vanishing nearby is specific to OUR side and can't coincidentally match a genuine mirror-deck opponent play the way card+timing alone could.

Re-verified against BOTH problem logs after these two fixes (not just the new one — re-ran the original problem log too, to confirm no regression there): misclassified count roughly quadrupled in both (log 1: 5->24 of 63; log 2: 2->11 of 31). Musketeer false positives dropped to ZERO in both logs. Giant false positives dropped sharply; spot-checked one remaining "giant played" (t=53.42, second log) and confirmed it's a genuine opponent play — no ally giant anywhere nearby, and the only nearby own-play was Mini P.E.K.K.A., not Giant — a useful reminder that not every remaining "played" event is a bug; Giant is genuinely central to this opponent's deck per Duncan's own read.

Remaining known gap, characterized but not chased further this round: a 2-archer burst still resolves "played" (misclassified, per Duncan) when the group's position has already crossed toward the opponent's side of the board (past the river) — the mirror-deck-safety position gate on the own-play-collision check deliberately declines to suppress anything not clearly on OUR side, which is correct for genuinely defending against a coincidental opponent mirror play, but also means it can't catch OUR OWN units that pushed deep into enemy territory (e.g. following a Giant) before getting momentarily misclassified. This is a real, structural limit of a position-snapshot heuristic, not a bug to patch — Duncan's own proposed direction (from the first round of this investigation) is the right one: better object permanence via the planned-but-not-started HP reading module, so death/occlusion/identity can be reasoned about directly instead of inferred from position snapshots.

Third live-match test (2026-08-16, partial match, log logs/debug_live_20260816_020953.jsonl) — Duncan's own read: "picking up almost nothing... this makes me think the issues lie largely with the visual interp model." Reported: spear_goblin wrongly claimed "played" (should at most be uncertain, since it's coming from the opponent's Goblin Hut), archers claimed "played" when Duncan hadn't played them and the opponent doesn't have them, opponent Giant/Knight plays not registering, and "another string of giants" appearing with almost nothing on the board. Also: Valkyrie was flagged "played" despite neither player having that card at all.

Investigated all four. Three turned out to be the SAME root cause, one is different and not fixable here:

The same-cause three: the previous round's SIDE_FLIP_WINDOW_SECONDS widening (to 2.0s) had an unintended side effect the previous round's testing didn't surface — it made the ally-vanish check trust ANY "ally"-labeled reading as a legitimate flip source, with no check on whether that reading itself was plausible. Traced the "string of giants" directly: an "ally" giant track (id 161) sat at bbox y~500-700 — deep in the OPPONENT's own territory, nowhere a real ally unit could legitimately be — for its entire life. This was itself a misclassification (a genuinely enemy giant mislabeled "ally" by an unreliable side.onnx read), but the tracker treated it as ground truth, so every SUBSEQUENT real appearance of that same enemy giant got wrongly explained away as "misclassified" — backwards from what the check exists to catch. The exact same mechanism explained the spear_goblin/goblin false "misclassified" hits from the previous round's over-wide window: goblin and spear_goblin aren't even in Duncan's deck, so any "ally" reading of them was already impossible on its face, yet nothing was checking that.

Fixed with two independent gates on trusting an "ally" reading as a real vanish source (game_knowledge/opponent_play_tracker.py's new _could_be_ours, checked before a vanish is ever recorded into _recently_vanished_ally): (1) deck-membership — OpponentPlayTracker now takes own_deck (wired from debug_live.py's already-loaded config/deck.txt); an "ally" reading of a card not in that deck is rejected outright, no ambiguity possible. (2) position plausibility — reusing RIVER_Y_TOP, an "ally" reading positioned clearly in the opponent's own territory is rejected as untrustworthy, regardless of card identity. Verified with two new dedicated tests (an impossible-card ally reading, and a bogus-position ally reading, both reproducing the real log cases) plus a full re-run of the existing suite (16 tests total now) before trusting it.

Re-verified against all three problem logs with the real deck passed in: goblin/spear_goblin never show "misclassified" now (impossible by construction), and genuine giant/knight plays that were being wrongly suppressed are "played" again. Real, understood trade-off from gate (2), confirmed by tracing one case directly: an ally musketeer that had legitimately pushed across the river into enemy territory (bbox y~527-617, well past RIVER_Y_TOP) before flipping no longer gets caught, since the position gate can't distinguish "a real ally unit that pushed forward" from "a bogus ally reading of a genuinely enemy unit" — both look identical from position alone. This is why musketeer/archer "played" counts went back up in the replay after this fix, not a regression — the alternative (trusting position-implausible ally readings) is what caused the worse bug this round. Real cost, correctly accepted: this needs the object-permanence/HP-tracking fix Duncan already proposed after the previous round to close properly, not more position heuristics.

The spear_goblin-from-Goblin-Hut concern (per Duncan's report) is the ALREADY-documented goblin_giant detection gap, not a new issue: spawn_data.py's SPAWN_TABLE only has goblin_hut -> goblin (confirmed correct by Duncan directly), not goblin_hut -> spear_goblin — the spear_goblin bursts are far more likely Goblin Giant's on-death 2x-spear_goblin spawn (a real card Duncan mentioned playing against), which can never be attributed to a source since goblin_giant isn't one of unit_detector.py's 97 detected classes. Single spear_goblin appearances default to "played" (no data says otherwise) and multi-unit bursts correctly land on "uncertain" (Duncan's own read: "correctly skeptical about the spear goblins") — this is the system behaving as designed given a real, unfixable-without-a-different-model gap, not a bug to chase further.

Valkyrie — different root cause, not fixable at this layer: neither player had Valkyrie in their deck at all, so this wasn't a side-classification issue (nothing in a deck-membership or position check would ever catch a card that's simply the WRONG CLASS from the start). units.onnx itself produced a wrong class label for whatever was actually on screen — a base object-detector accuracy limitation, same category as the earlier heal_spirit/inferno_tower non-fix. No code fix attempted; flagged here as a data point on Duncan's own hypothesis that some of the remaining noise really is the vision model itself, not the attribution logic on top of it.

Fourth live-match test (2026-08-16, log logs/debug_live_20260816_022347.jsonl) — still not working well, but this round's investigation found the practical ceiling of the heuristic-patching approach used in rounds 1-3, not just another instance of the same bug class.

Goblin Hut corrected AGAIN: the very first correction (round 1, before any code existed) said Goblin Hut -> goblin, not spear_goblin. Direct real-time observation this match (Duncan: "they were spawning from the hut") plus the log itself (a spear_goblin consistently appeared within ~85px and ~0.4s of the hut) both point the other way — reverted to Goblin Hut -> spear_goblin, the ORIGINAL first-draft guess. Not a logic bug, a data-correctness one; fixed in game_knowledge/spawn_data.py, documented with the full back-and-forth so it's not silently re-flipped a third time without cause. Confirms only 2 of 5 spear_goblin instances this match now correctly attribute to a hut — plausible, not a new problem, since the opponent placed 4 different Goblin Huts over the match (destroyed and replaced), so several spear_goblin appearances legitimately have no currently-tracked hut to attribute to.

The real finding: giant and musketeer misclassifications are STILL happening after three rounds of ally-vanish/position/deck-membership fixes, and this time traced all the way to root cause rather than patched again. Both an ally giant (track 95) and an ally musketeer (track 93) were found FLICKERING between "ally" and "enemy" almost every single frame — not a one-time flip, a sustained multi-times-per-second oscillation — while sitting deep in the opponent's own territory (both had pushed forward together, likely following each other on attack). This is qualitatively different from every previous case fixed so far (all of which were a single clean flip). No timing/position/deck heuristic can cleanly resolve a signal that's genuinely coin-flipping several times a second on the unit in question — every fix so far assumed misclassification was an occasional, identifiable EVENT; this is sustained unreliability for the full duration a unit spends past the river. Documented as the practical ceiling of this round's approach: closing this needs either improving side.onnx itself (the actual unreliable component) or the already-proposed object-permanence/HP-tracking system (Duncan's own suggestion from round 2) — not another downstream heuristic.

Two more findings, different root cause from the above, genuinely NOT fixable at this layer: (1) heal_spirit — now seen at the IDENTICAL bbox ([434-478, 80-120], immediately at the top edge of the arena crop) across three separate match logs. No longer a one-off — this is a persistent, location-specific false positive, a strong candidate for a small unit_detector.py detection-exclusion zone (same category as the existing tower false-positive fix), but needs visual confirmation of what's actually at that screen location before adding one blind (this project's own established rigor — verify visually before trusting a zone, per config/red_zones.py's own history). Not added without that. (2) goblin detection rate looks genuinely low — only 29 raw detections across 977 frames, average confidence 0.58 (versus e.g. giant/knight typically reading 0.95+) — consistent with Duncan's own hypothesis that a small green unit against green grass may be a harder case for units.onnx specifically. A base-detector recall/sensitivity limitation, not something the attribution layer downstream can compensate for. Also: the "phantom archer" Duncan reported (system read one archer, none were actually on screen) is the SAME category as the earlier Valkyrie finding — a units.onnx class-hallucination unrelated to side-classification; the attribution layer already handled it about as well as it can (stayed "uncertain," never escalated to "played," since a lone archer never matches the real 2-unit burst).

Follow-up same session: Duncan corrected two more things from the fourth match before deciding on direction — (1) Goblin Hut's spawn target flipped back to spear_goblin (see spawn_data.py's docstring for the full back-and-forth: first draft guessed spear_goblin, round-1 correction said goblin, this round's direct real-time observation plus log proximity/timing evidence both point back to spear_goblin — reverted, with the history documented so it isn't silently flipped a third time without cause). (2) The opponent did NOT place 4 Goblin Huts — Duncan's own count was 2; the other "played" events were re-reads of the same physical hut.

That second correction led to a genuinely new, different mechanism (not another round of the same troop-misclassification patching): buildings never move once placed, so three "goblin_hut played" events at bboxes within ~5px of each other for the whole match meant one hut's track kept getting lost (occlusion, confidence dips) and re-acquired as a "new" track_id well past what unit_tracker.py's own grace period could bridge — cleaner to detect than the troop-side flicker problem specifically because position tolerance can be very tight (a real building's bbox barely drifts at all) where troop-side checks have to tolerate real movement.

Added: BUILDING_CLASSES (12 genuinely-stationary classes — deliberately excludes goblin_drill and cannon_cart, neither is unambiguously static), a tight-position/long-time reacquisition check (BUILDING_REACQUIRE_PROXIMITY_PX=40 vs the troop checks' 150, BUILDING_REACQUIRE_WINDOW_SECONDS=8.0 — buildings don't have the "must still be nearby soon" time pressure a moving troop does), and a 4th-plus-one attribution value, "reacquired" (distinct from "spawned" — this isn't a byproduct of another card, it's literally the same building continuing to exist).

Real bug caught and fixed WHILE building this (not after, during testing — same rigor as every other fix this session): a brand-new building can flicker visible True->False->True within its own life, before its own candidate group even closes (GROUPING_WINDOW_SECONDS gives it time to) — that self-flicker was getting recorded as a "vanish" like any other, and without excluding a group's own track_id from the reacquisition search, a genuine FIRST-ever building placement matched against ITSELF and got wrongly reported "reacquired" instead of "played". Confirmed this happened for real in the log (the 4th, genuinely-real second hut, far from the first). Fixed by excluding a group's own track_ids from the search. Verified with 3 new dedicated tests (reacquisition of a real repeat, a genuine distant second building not swallowed, and the self-flicker case specifically) — 19 tests total now, all passing. Re-verified against the actual log: goblin_hut now reads played/reacquired/reacquired/played — 2 real plays, matching Duncan's own count exactly.

debug_live.py's panel updated to render "reacquired" distinctly (orange), and the module docstring updated to document it alongside the other three attribution values.

Fifth live-match test (2026-08-16, log logs/debug_live_20260816_024127.jsonl) — better (building fix confirmed working: goblin_hut read played/reacquired/reacquired/played, matching Duncan's real count of 2 exactly), but archers/goblin/giant/musketeer still an issue per Duncan. Found and fixed one more distinct mechanism; goblin and part of the remaining archer noise confirmed as genuinely unfixable at this layer.

New mechanism found and fixed: _find_ally_flip_source only ever looks at VANISHED ally tracks, but traced a real musketeer case where the ally track NEVER vanished — it stayed continuously alive and visible the entire time a conflicting "enemy" track of the same class was born and lived ~50px away. Two simultaneous conflicting reads of one physical unit, not a flip-then-vanish — a check built entirely around vanish events structurally cannot see this, no matter how its windows are tuned. Added `_find_concurrent_ally_duplicate`, searching currently-alive ally tracks near a new group instead of vanished ones.

Real design correction made while testing this (not after — same rigor as every fix this session): first version reused `_could_be_ours`'s position gate, and the test built directly from the real case's coordinates FAILED — the real ally musketeer had already pushed past the river, the same position blind spot already accepted for the vanish-based check. Realized position was only ever a proxy for trustworthiness because a vanished track has no live state left to check directly — a CURRENTLY-alive track's `age` field is direct, measured evidence of how established it is, no proxy needed. Redesigned around a minimum-age threshold (`CONCURRENT_DUPLICATE_MIN_AGE=10`, ~1s of continuous confirmation) instead, keeping only the unambiguous deck-membership half of the old gate (split out into its own `_class_in_own_deck` helper, now shared by both checks rather than duplicated). Verified with 2 new tests (the real case, reproduced exactly, plus a low-age "ally" correctly NOT triggering suppression) — 20 tests total now.

Re-verified against the log: musketeer false positives dropped to zero (was 1 played + the concurrent-duplicate case; now 0 played, 1 correctly misclassified). Giant dropped from 5 to 3 "played" (2 more correctly caught). Archer: 5 "played" x2-bursts remain — checked one directly, confirmed it has NO ally reading anywhere nearby at ANY point (not vanished, not concurrent) — the harder "born misclassified with nothing to correlate against" case already documented as a known limit of a heuristic-patching approach, not a new gap.

Duncan also asked directly: does anything currently look at hand-card selection (the visual "lift/enlarge" a card gets when tapped, before it's placed)? Checked the code to answer precisely rather than guess: no — config/hand_region.py's slot boxes are calibrated to the card's normal, unselected size only, and cycle_tracker.py only reacts AFTER a hand slot's content has already changed to a new card (i.e. after the play is already complete and the queue has advanced), not at the moment of selection. This is a real, currently-unused signal that would tighten OWN_PLAY_WINDOW_SECONDS's timing precision for the own-play-collision check specifically — but would NOT address the concurrent-duplicate/vanish-based misclassification cases (those are about the ENEMY-side reading being wrong, not about knowing our own timing better), and would not touch the goblin detection-rate issue or the archer no-correlatable-state case at all. Noted as a real, scoped enhancement candidate for later, not a fix for what's currently broken.

Goblin detection confirmed low again this match (consistent with the fourth match's finding) — Duncan: "never correctly seeing goblins." Still a units.onnx base-detector sensitivity limitation (small unit, possible green-on-green camouflage per Duncan's own theory), not something any attribution-layer logic can compensate for.

Sixth live-match test (2026-08-16, log logs/debug_live_20260816_114341.jsonl) — first test run against a Duncan-provided ordered ground-truth list of everything the opponent actually played (giant/goblin_hut/spear_goblin/giant+goblins/knight/spear_goblin/archers/giant/goblin_hut), not just impression-level review. Found and fixed one more real bug this way.

Bug: spear_goblin was STILL never resolving to "spawned" from goblin_hut despite the earlier goblin_hut->spear_goblin correction being correctly in spawn_data.py — traced directly: the hut's own track went fully untracked for ~8.2s right as it spawned a spear_goblin, just past BUILDING_REACQUIRE_WINDOW_SECONDS's old 8.0s cutoff, AND (the real structural bug) _find_spawn_source's periodic-spawner branch only ever checked currently-alive tracks, never falling back to the building's own recently-vanished record the way _find_building_reacquisition already does for the same building. Fixed both: widened the window to 45.0s (buildings like Goblin Hut have real multi-tens-of-seconds lifetimes — 8s was never matched to what it needed to bridge) and added the missing fallback path, scoped deliberately to BUILDING_CLASSES only (troop periodic-spawners like Witch keep requiring "currently alive," since a troop that stops being tracked may really have died, unlike a building). Verified with a new test reproducing the exact 8.2s gap (21 tests total now) plus the real log: spear_goblin "spawned" attributions went from 0 to 5.

Cross-checked the full replay against Duncan's ground-truth list: goblin_hut "played" = 2, matching his count exactly (left bridge + next to king tower). Archers "played" = 1, matching exactly (middle, his side). Giant and Knight still show more "played" events than his list mentions (8 vs 3 for Giant, 2 vs 1 for Knight) — can't fully resolve without exact timestamps to match against; plausibly some are real (Giant is clearly their repeated win condition, cycling roughly every 15-25s across a 138s match isn't an unreasonable real rate) rather than 100% residual false positives, but not confirmed either way. The regular "Goblins" (4-unit burst) card from Duncan's list never resolved cleanly — still just scattered single "uncertain" goblins — consistent with the already-documented detection-rate limitation, not a new gap.

Duncan also asked directly whether the system uses hand-card selection (the visual lift/enlarge a tapped card gets before placement) as a signal — checked the code to answer precisely: no, nothing currently does. config/hand_region.py's slot boxes are calibrated to the unselected size only, and cycle_tracker.py only reacts after a hand slot's content has already changed (after the play is complete), not at the moment of selection. Real, unused signal, but scoped: would only tighten OWN_PLAY_WINDOW_SECONDS's timing precision for the own-play-collision check specifically, not touch the concurrent-duplicate/vanish-based misclassification cases, the goblin detection-rate issue, or the archer no-correlatable-state case. Noted as a future enhancement candidate, not attempted this session.

