**Third live-match test of the position-priority fix (2026-08-18, same day, third round): found and fixed a much more fundamental bug in the priority-tier design itself — both fixes above were still live-tested and reported "still marking my units as enemies almost 100% of the time... after a tower is taken or after they cross the bridge."**

Duncan's precise correction to the design: position was never meant to be authoritative — it's the system's FIRST GUESS, refined by the spawn icon, and both should always be correctable by the badge (color-coded level number + health bar), which he called "the #1 most consistent marker our system had... perfectly... before" — proven "almost 100% accurate" on its own before position/icon existed. The bug: the previous `_resolve_side` let position/icon PERMANENTLY seed a track (`side_seeded=True`, never revisited again) — so a wrong position guess (any legitimate cross-river push, or the still-untracked neutral-zone case) locked a track wrong for its entire life, with the badge never getting a second chance even once it started reading a clean, correct, repeated "ally" signal.

**Redesigned around explicit priority TIERS instead of a hard lock**: `TIER_POSITION(1) < TIER_ICON(2) < TIER_BADGE(3)` in `perception/unit_tracker.py`. Position/icon can only ever write a tier at or above their own rank (so a lower tier can never clobber a higher one), but the badge is checked EVERY frame regardless of current tier or track age, and whenever its own `SIDE_LOCK_HITS=2` debounce is satisfied, it unconditionally overwrites whatever tier was there before — the mechanism that actually lets a real, repeated ally badge reading correct an earlier wrong "enemy" position/icon guess.

**A second, deeper bug was found WHILE implementing this and fixed in the same pass, before it could ship broken**: `_side_compatible()` (used at the MATCHING step, before `_resolve_side` ever runs) was still unconditionally rejecting any detection whose known side conflicted with a track's current side — meaning the very detection that would carry the correcting badge reading would already be thrown out before the new tier logic ever got a chance to run, silently defeating the entire redesign. Fixed: `_side_compatible` now allows a conflicting detection to match as long as the track's current side is still below `TIER_BADGE` (a provisional guess, not yet confirmed) — only a track that's ALREADY reached `TIER_BADGE` keeps the original strict behavior (a conflicting reading can't match at all, spins up a separate track instead), so an already-confirmed track can't be casually reopened by one disagreeing frame, while a still-provisional one can be corrected.

Verified with 2 new synthetic tests, including the core regression case: a track locked "enemy" by position, then 2 consecutive real "ally" badge reads, correctly ends at "ally" (`perception/test_unit_tracker.py`.`test_badge_corrects_a_wrong_position_lock_across_the_river`) — plus a rewritten icon test confirming a single conflicting badge frame does NOT flip an icon-set side (still needs its own debounce) while 2 consecutive ones DO. 11 tests total, all passing, plus the full existing cross-module suite (opponent-tracker's 22 tests, spawn_icon_reader's real-capture check) re-verified with no regressions.

