**Perception fusion architecture — design doc + first implementation wave (2026-08-19/20)**

Duncan wrote a standalone design doc ("Perception fusion architecture — layering the 5 board-state systems," revised 3 times same day) reframing how the 5 perception systems (YOLO detection, tracking, team ID, spawn icon, spawn-location legality) should combine: not competing judges producing final answers that get reconciled, but ONE shared per-track record that different systems contribute evidence to at different moments — track identity resolved before any attribute logic runs, and spawn-location zone classification treated as DETERMINISTIC GROUND TRUTH in two of three zone types (not just "evidence"), once princess-tower alive/destroyed state is known per-lane. Full doc content is in conversation history, not reproduced here. Key corrections made to `unit_tracker.py`'s existing design during review:
- The tier system's TIER_POSITION was really "first guess," and a POLARIZED zone (per the doc) makes position not just a strong guess but *proof* — a correction still pending the tower-state-reader dependency below, not yet implemented.
- Class (unit name) needed the same accumulate-don't-overwrite treatment already given to side — implemented, see below.
- Scope decision made explicitly with Duncan on the doc's proposed Kalman+IoU+Hungarian tracker upgrade: NOT built. Simple linear-velocity extrapolation was chosen instead after Duncan correctly pushed back on an early claim that CR troop motion is "near-constant speed, fairly direct" — true only for a narrow slice of building-targeting win-conditions; most units retarget onto the nearest enemy unpredictably, and a Kalman filter's constant-velocity process model has the exact same blind spot to that as simple extrapolation does. What actually matters for the retargeting concern isn't the motion model at all — it's a card-type-aware search-radius SHAPE, which was built (see below) using real per-card `Target` data instead of guessing at CR mechanics from memory.

**`game_knowledge/card_reference.json` created** — a 122-card reference file (programmatically generated, not hand-typed) for Duncan to fill in movement-speed tiers over time, plus lower-priority combat stats (hit speed, range, sight range, HP, damage) flagged as "nice to have, not blocking." Pre-filled from data already trusted in this project: `elixir_cost`/`rarity` from `config/cards.json`, `detected_class` cross-referenced against `unit_detector.py`'s 97 classes + `game_knowledge/spawn_data.py`'s existing slug mappings (handling Musketeer/Three Musketeers, Skeletons/Skeleton Army, Rascals' dual classes, Elixir Golem/Phoenix's stage classes), `category`/`transport`/`target_type` from `vendor/build-a-bot`'s `Target`/`Transport`/`UnitCategory` data. Real findings surfaced while building it, not yet acted on: **7 cards couldn't be auto-classified at all** (Boss Bandit, Goblin Curse, Goblin Demolisher, Goblin Machine, Goblinstein, Rune Giant, Vines — possibly newer releases beyond the already-documented 6-card detection gap, left `null` rather than guessed); **Goblin Gang's exact spawn composition** wasn't asserted (flagged for Duncan directly, same rigor as the Goblin Hut spawn-target history); and a **real unfixed gap in `spawn_data.py`'s `CARD_BURST_COUNT`** was found for Minion Horde (6 minions, distinct from the 3-minion Minions card), Royal Hogs, Skeleton Dragons, and Wall Breakers — same class of bug the Musketeer 1-vs-3 special case already handles, just not yet extended to these. Not fixed, flagged in each entry's own `notes` field.

