**`perception/unit_tracker.py` upgraded with two real features, both synthetically tested, NEITHER live-tested yet:**

1. **Class accumulator** — matching no longer hard-requires exact class-name equality (only side-compatibility + being within the track's search area); a track's reported class is instead resolved by a new debounce (`CLASS_LOCK_HITS=2`, mirrors `SIDE_LOCK_HITS`) via `_resolve_class`. Fixes the specific failure mode where a single misclassified frame on an established track (documented Valkyrie/spear_goblin↔archer hallucinations) failed to match anything and spun up a phantom candidate track — does NOT fix a base-model hallucination sustained across multiple consecutive frames (that's a real `units.onnx` accuracy limit, not a tracking-layer bug).
2. **Velocity-based, target-type-aware reacquisition** — the direct fix for the already-documented "troop-tracking-gap double-counting" bug. Each track keeps an EMA-smoothed velocity estimate (`_update_velocity`); once a track misses a frame, matching uses its velocity-extrapolated PREDICTED position (`_predicted_center`) instead of its frozen last-known one, with the search area's SHAPE depending on `perception/unit_metadata.py`'s new `TARGET_TYPE`/`targets_buildings_only()` (sourced from the same vendor `Target` data, Giant-corrected): a predictable building-targeter with a clear velocity gets a TIGHT, DIRECTIONAL ellipse along its own heading (`_search_radius_ok`); everything else (the common case — most units retarget unpredictably) gets a WIDE circular fallback. Radius constants are first-pass placeholders, explicitly meant to be refined once `card_reference.json`'s `movement_speed` field is filled in (not blocking — a generic radius is used until then).

Verified with 5 new synthetic tests (`perception/test_unit_tracker.py`, now 16 total, all passing): a single misclassified frame keeps the same track_id without relabeling it; 2 consistent different reads DO relabel it; a building-targeter reacquires far along its own predicted heading; the SAME building-targeter does NOT reacquire if it reappears sideways of that heading (real building-targeters don't retarget); a non-building unit DOES reacquire via the wide fallback even with that same sideways deviation. Full existing cross-module suite (opponent-tracker's 22 tests, spawn_icon_reader's real-capture check) re-verified with no regressions.

**Not yet live-tested — this is genuinely new tracker machinery (velocity model + search-area shaping), not a small patch. Needs a real match before trusting it.**

**Tower-state reader built and live-tested against real ground truth (2026-08-20) — `perception/tower_reader.py` and `perception/test_tower_reader.py` (5 tests, all passing).**

Confirmed early: `vendor/build-a-bot`'s tower-HP data wasn't directly usable (calibrated at a different resolution — 368×652 vs. this project's 900×1600 — and only covers the 4 princess towers, not either king tower) — only the two-color bar-fill-scan *algorithm* (same style `elixir_counter.py` already validates) was reusable. Everything else needed a real calibration session (Duncan capture + `find_coords.py`, same pattern as every other calibrated feature in this project).

Two real capture sessions: (1) a fresh match, all 4 princess towers near full health — calibrated regions + filled-bar colors, verified all 4 read ~1.00. (2) A later match capture with both king towers damaged (Duncan: ally ~69%, enemy ~25%) and both LEFT princess towers destroyed. Several real bugs found and fixed along the way, all before shipping, not after:

1. **Row-selection picked the wrong row twice** before landing on a working design — first a background/border row (a "least digit-contaminated" heuristic scored FOR the absence of digit-like brightness, not FOR actually looking like the bar), then a digit-blended row (a looser hue-family mask admitted anti-aliased digit-edge pixels). Fixed by scoring rows on proximity to the SPECIFIC calibrated filled/empty colors, not just the right hue family.
2. **The original "destroyed" heuristic was built backwards.** It assumed a destroyed tower would read a very-low-but-still-colored fill fraction; direct inspection of the real destroyed-tower capture (per Duncan) showed a destroyed tower has literally NO UI element at all — no bar, no number, just rubble. Redesigned around that: `read_tower()` is now a simple stateless per-frame reader (`hp_fraction` or `None`, no "destroyed" claim at all — a single frame can't tell "destroyed" apart from "temporarily unreadable"), and a new `TowerStateTracker` class debounces a SUSTAINED streak of `None` (`DESTROYED_CONFIRM_FRAMES=5`, mirrors `SIDE_LOCK_HITS`/`HAND_STABLE_FRAMES`) into a confirmed, permanent `destroyed` — a lone miss doesn't count.
3. **A real design gap caught mid-implementation, before it shipped**: Duncan corrected an earlier statement — a king tower's bar doesn't render until EITHER it's taken direct damage OR a princess tower on that same side has been destroyed. That means a perfectly healthy, not-yet-activated king tower produces the EXACT SAME "no clean row" signal as a genuinely destroyed one. A first fix (`_ever_read` gate, only start the destroyed-miss-streak after a tower's bar has been read at least once) was applied UNIVERSALLY at first, which broke princess-tower detection instead — a princess tower already destroyed from frame 1 would never pass `_ever_read` either and could never be confirmed destroyed. Fixed by scoping the gate to KING towers only (princess towers have no equivalent "not yet activated" state — their bar is always visible from frame 1 if alive). A `continue` statement in the fix's first draft also silently skipped populating that tower's own result dict entirely — caught by the test suite itself failing with a `KeyError`, not assumed correct.
4. **King tower bars turned out to render with a highlight/shadow gradient across their own height** (two visually different bands both encoding the SAME horizontal fill transition, not a filled-vs-empty split) — the existing best-row selection design handled this without needing to know about it explicitly, since it just picks whichever band's colors most closely match the calibrated reference.

**Real validation, the strongest of anything in this module**: the king-tower fill-fraction calculation, worked out from real pixel color transitions before any code was written, came out to ~68%/~24% against Duncan's own reported ~69%/~25% — an independent, close match to real ground truth, not just internal self-consistency. `FILLED_BGR` turned out nearly identical between king and princess towers (same underlying UI color); `EMPTY_BGR` did not, confirming they're worth calibrating independently (kept in a dict keyed by `(tower_type, side)`).

**Per-lane zone classification built and wired in (2026-08-20), same session — the actual payoff of the tower reader above.**

New `game_knowledge/zone_classifier.py` (7 tests, all passing) implements the fusion doc's 3-zone-type model: `classify_zone(x, y, tower_state)` returns `"ally"`/`"enemy"`/`"neutral"`/`"unknown"`, given `TowerStateTracker`'s own state dict. Geometry: one REAL zone, click-calibrated via `find_coords.py` against a capture with the enemy's own left princess tower destroyed — `(65, 501, 450, 711)` — everything else (`config/red_zones.py`'s `ENEMY_RIGHT_NEUTRAL_ZONE`/`ALLY_LEFT_NEUTRAL_ZONE`/`ALLY_RIGHT_NEUTRAL_ZONE`) is DERIVED by symmetry, not independently measured: the left/right mirror was Duncan's own explicit instruction ("this should let you flip it to find the right princess tower zone as well"); the ally/enemy (vertical) mirror is a reasonable but unconfirmed inference from board symmetry, flagged for verification if a real ally-tower-destroyed capture ever becomes available.

