Profiled rather than guessed. Two separate causes, NEITHER of which is the new detector:

1. CHOPPINESS — hand-card matching, ~48ms/frame, more than double the GPU detector's
   ~21ms (tower_reader 1.5ms, spawn icons 0.4ms). It was re-identifying cards every
   frame when a hand only actually changes on a play. Two fixes applied:
   - debug_live now re-matches at CARD_MATCH_INTERVAL (0.2s, ~5Hz) and reuses the last
     result between. Boxes still draw every frame so the overlay doesn't flicker.
     cycle_tracker is updated ONLY on frames with a fresh match — feeding it the cached
     tuple repeatedly would let identical values satisfy its HAND_STABLE_FRAMES debounce
     with no new evidence, the exact false-confirmation that debounce prevents.
   - CORRECTION, same day: I first reported config/deck.txt as EMPTY and told Duncan to
     fill it in for an ~8x win. That was WRONG — I ran `head -8`, saw only the comment
     header, and concluded from that without reading the rest. The file has all 8 cards
     and always has. He caught it.
     The REAL cost driver: assets/hand_templates/ contains ONLY those 8 deck cards, at
     ~40 examples EACH (321 total). Deck restriction therefore filters nothing — the
     library is already restricted. Cost scales with examples PER CARD, not card count.
     If matching needs to get cheaper beyond the throttle above, the lever is capping
     examples per card at match time (e.g. the N most recently added, or a clustered
     subset) — MAX_EXAMPLES_PER_CARD=40 was chosen for collection breadth, and there is
     no evidence all 40 are needed for accurate MATCHING. Untested; measure before
     assuming it's safe.

2. DELAY BEHIND THE GAME — not our loop. capture.py never queues (a background thread
   overwrites _latest_frame; screenshot() always takes the freshest). Lowered
   adbnativeblitz's screenshotbuffer 5 -> 2, which is pure latency we don't use. The
   larger, irreducible part is that `adb shell screenrecord` is an H.264 ENCODER and
   buffers before emitting (~100-300ms). That's the cost of the ~54fps that replaced
   ~1.5fps screencap; removing it means a different backend (scrcpy) — a much bigger
   change. Cosmetic for a debug view (every module reads the same frame, so analysis
   stays self-consistent) but a REAL problem in Phase 5 when the bot acts on what it sees.

